{"name":"Dark Ages","hex":"#9698a3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Dark Ages","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The period of European history encompassing (roughly) 476–1000 CE.","The Greek Dark Ages (c. 1100–750 BCE)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Dark_Ages"}],"entries":[{"title":"Dark Ages (historiography)","blocks":[{"text":"The Dark Ages is a term, now deprecated by most historians, for the Early Middle Ages, or occasionally the entire Middle Ages, in Western Europe after the fall of the Western Roman Empire, which characterises it as marked by economic, intellectual, and cultural decline."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Petrarch_by_Bargilla.jpg/330px-Petrarch_by_Bargilla.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dark_Ages_(historiography)"},{"title":"Early Middle Ages","blocks":[{"text":"The Early Middle Ages, sometimes controversially referred to as the Dark Ages, is typically regarded by historians as lasting from the late 5th to the 10th century. They marked the start of the Middle Ages of European history, following the decline of the Western Roman Empire, and preceding the High Middle Ages. The alternative term late antiquity, for the early part of the period, emphasizes elements of continuity with the Roman Empire, while Early Middle Ages is used to emphasize developments characteristic of the earlier medieval period."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Codex_Aureus_Sankt_Emmeram.jpg/330px-Codex_Aureus_Sankt_Emmeram.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Early_Middle_Ages"},{"title":"Darkness","blocks":[{"text":"Darkness is the condition resulting from a lack of illumination, or an absence of visible light."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Creation_of_Light.png/330px-Creation_of_Light.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Darkness"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Tommaso.Laureti_Triumph.of.Christianity.jpg/330px-Tommaso.Laureti_Triumph.of.Christianity.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Tommaso.Laureti_Triumph.of.Christianity.jpg/330px-Tommaso.Laureti_Triumph.of.Christianity.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Triumph of Christianity by Tommaso Laureti (1530–1602), ceiling painting in the Sala di Constantino, Vatican Palace. Images like this one celebrate the triumph of Christianity over the paganism of Antiquity.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATommaso.Laureti_Triumph.of.Christianity.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/European_Output_of_Manuscripts_500%E2%80%931500.png/500px-European_Output_of_Manuscripts_500%E2%80%931500.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/European_Output_of_Manuscripts_500%E2%80%931500.png/500px-European_Output_of_Manuscripts_500%E2%80%931500.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Medieval production of manuscripts.[29] The beginning of the Middle Ages was also a period of low activity in copying. This graph does not include the Byzantine Empire.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEuropean_Output_of_Manuscripts_500%E2%80%931500.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Gossuin_de_Metz_-_L%27image_du_monde_-_BNF_Fr._574_fo42_-_miniature.jpg/500px-Gossuin_de_Metz_-_L%27image_du_monde_-_BNF_Fr._574_fo42_-_miniature.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail